  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone may be associated with Corneal Ulcer through a series of indirect pathways involving inflammatory conditions. Specifically, Hydrocortisone is linked to Iritis, which in turn is associated with Fluorometholone or Medrysone, both of which are indicated to treat Corneal Ulcer. These relationships are supported by a consistent score of 0.5571 across three distinct paths, indicating a potential functional or pharmacological link between Hydrocortisone and Corneal Ulcer. The mechanisms appear to involve the modulation of inflammatory responses, as these drugs are commonly used in the treatment of uveitis and iritis, which are inflammatory eye conditions. The overlapping pathways suggest that Hydrocortisone may exert a therapeutic effect on corneal pathology through systemic or localized inflammatory regulation. However, the evidence is limited to the indicated relationships and does not provide direct evidence of a causal relationship between Hydrocortisone and Corneal Ulcer. The strength of the evidence is moderate, and further studies would be needed to confirm the role of Hydrocortisone in the development of corneal ulceration.",
